DEEP[1]=("Chapter 10 is named Vibhūti Yoga, the yoga of divine glories. Arjuna asked nothing at the end of chapter 9; Kṛṣṇa simply goes on, as a friend keeps talking to one who loves to listen.",
 "<i>Bhūya eva mahā-bāho</i>, “once more, mighty-armed one”. <i>Śṛṇu me paramaṃ vacaḥ</i>, “hear my supreme word”. <i>Yat te’haṃ prīyamāṇāya</i>, “which to you, who are delighted”. <i>Vakṣyāmi hita-kāmyayā</i>, “I will speak, wishing your good”. Commentators note <i>prīyamāṇa</i>, “delighting”: Arjuna’s joy in hearing draws out more teaching. Śaṅkara says the Lord teaches out of concern for Arjuna’s welfare. Rāmānuja says the Lord, seeing Arjuna’s love, longs to reveal more of his glory. Chapter 10 is in a sense the Lord speaking about himself to a friend, the way one shares what one loves. It prepares the ground for the vision of chapter 11.",
 "A willing listener draws out the best in a teacher. Bring delight, not just duty, to what you learn.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Na me viduḥ sura-gaṇāḥ</i>, “the hosts of gods do not know”. <i>Prabhavaṃ na maharṣayaḥ</i>, “nor the great sages, my origin”. <i>Aham ādir hi devānāṃ</i>, “for I am the source of the gods”. <i>Maharṣīṇāṃ ca sarvaśaḥ</i>, “and of the great sages, in every way”.",
 "Commentators read <i>prabhava</i> as “origin” or “coming forth”: how the Lord manifests. The Ṛg Veda’s Nāsadīya Sūkta (10.129) asks who knows how creation came about; even the gods came later, so who can tell? Commentators link the two passages. Śaṅkara says what is the source cannot be fully known by its products. Rāmānuja says even the gods cannot grasp the Lord’s greatness, only aspects of it. The verse sets a tone of humility for the whole chapter: what follows are glimpses, not a complete account.",
 "The deepest source is always larger than our grasp. Humility is not weakness; it is accuracy.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Yo mām ajam anādiṃ ca</i>, “one who knows me as unborn and beginningless”. <i>Vetti loka-maheśvaram</i>, “the great Lord of the worlds”. <i>Asammūḍhaḥ sa martyeṣu</i>, “he is undeluded among mortals”. <i>Sarva-pāpaiḥ pramucyate</i>, “and is freed from all sins”.",
 "<i>Aja</i>, unborn, and <i>anādi</i>, beginningless, recall 4.6: the Lord takes birth but is unborn. Commentators note that knowing this frees one from sin. Why? Śaṅkara: because knowing the Lord as the Self removes the ignorance that causes sinful action. Rāmānuja: because such knowledge produces devotion, and devotion purifies. <i>Asammūḍha</i>, undeluded, is a strong word: among people confused about who they are and where the world comes from, such a person sees clearly.",
 "Knowing there is a source beyond us brings both humility and a quiet freedom.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Buddhir jñānam asammohaḥ</i>, “intelligence, knowledge, freedom from delusion”. <i>Kṣamā satyaṃ damaḥ śamaḥ</i>, “forbearance, truth, self-control, calm”. <i>Sukhaṃ duḥkhaṃ bhavo’bhāvo</i>, “pleasure, pain, birth, death”. <i>Bhayaṃ cābhayam eva ca</i>, “fear and fearlessness too”.",
 "Verses 4–5 list twenty states of beings. Commentators note that virtues and their opposites appear side by side: pleasure and pain, birth and death, fear and fearlessness, fame and infamy. Śaṅkara says all arise from the Lord according to the karma of beings. Rāmānuja says the Lord gives each state as fits each being’s past actions. <i>Kṣamā</i>, forbearance or forgiveness, and <i>satya</i>, truth, are two of the most praised virtues in Indian ethics. <i>Dama</i> and <i>śama</i>, control of senses and of mind, are the first steps in Vedānta practice.",
 "Even difficult feelings arise from the same source as joyful ones. Each has something to teach.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Ahiṃsā samatā tuṣṭis</i>, “non-violence, equanimity, contentment”. <i>Tapo dānaṃ yaśo’yaśaḥ</i>, “austerity, charity, fame, infamy”. <i>Bhavanti bhāvā bhūtānāṃ</i>, “these states of beings arise”. <i>Matta eva pṛthag-vidhāḥ</i>, “from me alone, in their variety”.",
 "<i>Ahiṃsā</i>, non-violence, heads the second line. Patañjali places it first among the <i>yamas</i> (Yoga Sūtra 2.30), and Jain and Buddhist ethics give it central place. Gandhi made it the core of his politics. Commentators note that the Gītā names it here as a divine quality, even while urging Arjuna to fight a just war. They reconcile the two: ahiṃsā is the inner absence of hatred, which a righteous warrior can keep. <i>Samatā</i>, equanimity, and <i>tuṣṭi</i>, contentment, are key words of earlier chapters. The list ends with fame and infamy, both from the Lord.",
 "Cultivate the good qualities on this list, and remember where they come from.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Maharṣayaḥ sapta pūrve</i>, “the seven great sages of old”. <i>Catvāro manavas tathā</i>, “and the four Manus” or “the four, and the Manus”. <i>Mad-bhāvā mānasā jātā</i>, “filled with my nature, born of my mind”. <i>Yeṣāṃ loka imāḥ prajāḥ</i>, “from whom are these creatures of the world”.",
 "The seven great sages, <i>saptarṣi</i>, are often listed as Marīci, Atri, Aṅgiras, Pulastya, Pulaha, Kratu and Vasiṣṭha; lists vary. The four earlier ones may be the Kumāras: Sanaka, Sanandana, Sanātana and Sanatkumāra, eternal child-sages. The Manus are the progenitors of humanity, fourteen in each kalpa. Commentators differ on how to divide the phrase. Śaṅkara takes “four Manus”. Rāmānuja and others take “the four (Kumāras) and the Manus”. All agree the point: every lineage traces back to the Lord’s mind. The seven stars of the Great Bear are named after the seven sages.",
 "Every family tree, however long, goes back to one source. In that sense, all people are kin.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Etāṃ vibhūtiṃ yogaṃ ca</i>, “this glory and power”. <i>Mama yo vetti tattvataḥ</i>, “of mine, who knows truly”. <i>So’vikampena yogena</i>, “by unshaking yoga”. <i>Yujyate nātra saṃśayaḥ</i>, “is united; no doubt here”.",
 "<i>Vibhūti</i>, divine glory or manifestation, names the chapter. <i>Yoga</i> here means the Lord’s power, as in 9.5 (<i>yogam aiśvaram</i>). Commentators explain the link: when one sees the Divine in all that is excellent, devotion becomes steady, <i>avikampa</i>, unshaking. Śaṅkara says knowing the Lord’s glory leads to firm knowledge. Rāmānuja says it leads to firm bhakti. The word <i>vibhūti</i> also means sacred ash in Śaiva practice, worn as a sign of the divine; the shared meaning is “divine presence made visible”.",
 "Look for the Divine in what is excellent around you: a skilled hand, a kind word, a great tree. It steadies the heart.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Ahaṃ sarvasya prabhavo</i>, “I am the origin of all”. <i>Mattaḥ sarvaṃ pravartate</i>, “from me everything proceeds”. <i>Iti matvā bhajante māṃ</i>, “knowing this, they worship me”. <i>Budhā bhāva-samanvitāḥ</i>, “the wise, full of feeling”.",
 "Verses 10.8–11 are called the <i>catuḥ-ślokī</i>, the four-verse essence of the Gītā, especially in the Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇava tradition of Caitanya. Commentators note that <i>bhāva</i>, deep feeling or love, is joined to <i>budha</i>, wisdom: the wise are not dry. Śaṅkara reads <i>bhāva</i> as an earnest longing for the highest. Rāmānuja reads it as intense love. Madhva stresses knowledge of the Lord’s supremacy as the ground of love. The verse links knowledge and devotion: knowing that everything comes from the Lord, the wise love him.",
 "Understanding and love strengthen each other. The more you understand, the more you can love.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Mac-cittā mad-gata-prāṇā</i>, “minds on me, lives given to me”. <i>Bodhayantaḥ parasparam</i>, “enlightening one another”. <i>Kathayantaś ca māṃ nityaṃ</i>, “and always speaking of me”. <i>Tuṣyanti ca ramanti ca</i>, “they are content and delight”.",
 "This verse describes <i>satsaṅga</i>, the company of the good. Commentators note the mutual teaching: <i>bodhayantaḥ parasparam</i>, “waking each other up”. Śaṅkara says such devotees find contentment in talk of the Lord. Rāmānuja says they cannot live without speaking of him, like lovers who speak only of the beloved. <i>Tuṣyanti</i> and <i>ramanti</i>: contentment and joy. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa describes the gopīs of Vraja speaking of Kṛṣṇa in this way. Devotional gatherings, from village bhajan groups to Gītā study circles, draw on this verse.",
 "Find company that lifts you, and share what inspires you. Joy grows when shared.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Teṣāṃ satata-yuktānāṃ</i>, “to those ever united”. <i>Bhajatāṃ prīti-pūrvakam</i>, “worshipping with love”. <i>Dadāmi buddhi-yogaṃ taṃ</i>, “I give the yoga of understanding”. <i>Yena mām upayānti te</i>, “by which they come to me”.",
 "<i>Buddhi-yoga</i>, the yoga of understanding, was first named in 2.49. Here it is given by the Lord as grace. Commentators note the order: love first, then understanding is given. Śaṅkara says the Lord gives the knowledge of the Self. Rāmānuja says the Lord gives the understanding needed to reach him. The verse reassures those who feel unable to understand difficult teachings: love, kept up steadily, brings understanding. <i>Prīti-pūrvakam</i>, “with love”, is the key.",
 "If something seems hard to understand, keep loving and practising. Understanding often arrives as a gift.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Teṣām evānukampārtham</i>, “out of compassion for them alone”. <i>Aham ajñāna-jaṃ tamaḥ</i>, “I, the darkness born of ignorance”. <i>Nāśayāmy ātma-bhāva-stho</i>, “destroy, dwelling in their hearts”. <i>Jñāna-dīpena bhāsvatā</i>, “with the shining lamp of knowledge”.",
 "This image is beloved across traditions: the Lord within lights a lamp in the dark heart. Commentators note <i>anukampā</i>, compassion: the motive is love, not reward. <i>Ātma-bhāva-sthaḥ</i>, “dwelling in their inner being”. Śaṅkara says the lamp is discriminating knowledge, fed by devotion, fanned by meditation. Rāmānuja says the Lord himself removes ignorance by showing his own nature. The Upaniṣadic prayer <i>tamaso mā jyotir gamaya</i>, “lead me from darkness to light” (Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.3.28), is answered here.",
 "In dark moments, remember there is a lamp within. Sit quietly and let it brighten.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Paraṃ brahma paraṃ dhāma</i>, “supreme Brahman, supreme abode”. <i>Pavitraṃ paramaṃ bhavān</i>, “supreme purifier are you”. <i>Puruṣaṃ śāśvataṃ divyam</i>, “the eternal, divine Person”. <i>Ādi-devam ajaṃ vibhum</i>, “the first God, unborn, all-pervading”.",
 "Arjuna now speaks, not to ask but to praise. Commentators note the change since chapter 1, where he saw only family and omens. Each title echoes something earlier: <i>param brahma</i> (8.3), <i>param dhāma</i> (8.21), <i>pavitram</i> (9.2), <i>puruṣa</i> (8.8–10), <i>aja</i> (4.6, 10.3). Arjuna has been listening. Śaṅkara says Arjuna praises the Lord as the supreme reality. Rāmānuja says he praises the Lord’s personal greatness. Praise, <i>stuti</i>, is a natural outflow of understanding.",
 "When you truly understand something good, praise follows naturally. Let gratitude speak.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Āhus tvām ṛṣayaḥ sarve</i>, “all the sages say this of you”. <i>Devarṣir nāradas tathā</i>, “the divine sage Nārada”. <i>Asito devalo vyāsaḥ</i>, “Asita, Devala and Vyāsa”. <i>Svayaṃ caiva bravīṣi me</i>, “and you yourself tell me”.",
 "Nārada is the wandering divine sage who plays the vīṇā and sings the Lord’s names. Asita and Devala are sometimes named as father and son, or one person called Asita Devala. Vyāsa is the compiler of the Vedas and the Mahābhārata itself. In the Sabhā Parva, these sages praised Kṛṣṇa at Yudhiṣṭhira’s great sacrifice. Commentators note that Arjuna cites two kinds of evidence: the testimony of the wise, <i>āpta-vākya</i>, and Kṛṣṇa’s own word. Indian philosophy counts reliable testimony, <i>śabda</i>, as a valid means of knowledge.",
 "When the wise across generations agree, and your own experience confirms it, faith becomes firm.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Sarvam etad ṛtaṃ manye</i>, “I accept all this as true”. <i>Yan māṃ vadasi keśava</i>, “that you tell me, Keśava”. <i>Na hi te bhagavan vyaktiṃ</i>, “for, Lord, your manifestation”. <i>Vidur devā na dānavāḥ</i>, “neither gods nor demons know”.",
 "<i>Ṛta</i>, truth or cosmic order, is an old Vedic word. Commentators note that Arjuna now calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Bhagavān</i>, Lord, a sign of his growing reverence. The contrast with 1.31, where he saw bad omens, and 2.7, where he was confused, is clear. Śaṅkara says Arjuna accepts without doubt. Rāmānuja says Arjuna knows the Lord’s greatness by his grace. The verse also prepares the paradox of the next: if no one knows the Lord, how does Arjuna? By the Lord’s self-revelation.",
 "Trust grows step by step as understanding deepens. There is no need to rush it.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Svayam evātmanātmānaṃ</i>, “you yourself, by yourself, yourself”. <i>Vettha tvaṃ puruṣottama</i>, “know, Supreme Person”. <i>Bhūta-bhāvana bhūteśa</i>, “source of beings, Lord of beings”. <i>Deva-deva jagat-pate</i>, “God of gods, Lord of the world”.",
 "Five titles in one line, an outpouring of reverence. <i>Puruṣottama</i>, Supreme Person, is explained in 15.16–18. Commentators note the threefold repetition of “self”: only the Lord knows the Lord, by himself. Śaṅkara says the Lord’s self-knowledge is his very nature. Rāmānuja says others know him only as he chooses to reveal himself. The verse prepares Arjuna’s request in the next verse. If only you know yourself, then only you can tell me.",
 "Some things can be known only as a gift from the one who knows. Be open to receive.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Vaktum arhasy aśeṣeṇa</i>, “please tell fully”. <i>Divyā hy ātma-vibhūtayaḥ</i>, “your divine glories”. <i>Yābhir vibhūtibhir lokān</i>, “by which glories, the worlds”. <i>Imāṃs tvaṃ vyāpya tiṣṭhasi</i>, “you pervade and abide in”.",
 "Commentators note Arjuna’s eagerness: devotion wants to know more about the beloved. Śaṅkara says Arjuna wants to know the glories for meditation. Rāmānuja says he longs to hear of the Lord’s greatness for its own sake. The request leads to the long list of 10.20–39. It also leads, in chapter 11, to the request to see the cosmic form. Arjuna’s requests grow bolder as his trust grows.",
 "Love is curious about the beloved. Let your curiosity about the sacred grow.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Kathaṃ vidyām ahaṃ yogiṃs</i>, “how may I know you, yogi”. <i>Tvāṃ sadā paricintayan</i>, “always meditating on you”. <i>Keṣu keṣu ca bhāveṣu</i>, “and in what various forms”. <i>Cintyo’si bhagavan mayā</i>, “are you to be thought of by me, Lord”.",
 "Arjuna asks a practical question: what should I think of, to keep you in mind? Commentators note that he calls Kṛṣṇa <i>yogin</i>, master of yoga. Śaṅkara says the vibhūtis are supports for meditation for those not yet able to meditate on the formless. Rāmānuja says they show the Lord’s presence in all excellent things, for constant remembrance. The answer turns the whole world into a reminder of the Divine. This is one of the most practical aids to devotion in the Gītā.",
 "Choose a few daily reminders of the sacred: the sun at dawn, a tree, a kind face. Let them call you back.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Vistareṇātmano yogaṃ</i>, “in detail, your power”. <i>Vibhūtiṃ ca janārdana</i>, “and glory, Janārdana”. <i>Bhūyaḥ kathaya tṛptir hi</i>, “tell again, for satisfaction”. <i>Śṛṇvato nāsti me’mṛtam</i>, “I have none, hearing your nectar”.",
 "<i>Amṛta</i>, nectar or the drink of immortality. Commentators note that Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa to repeat what he has said in chapters 7 and 9, and to expand it. Śaṅkara says the words are like nectar because they lead to immortality. Rāmānuja says the devotee never tires of hearing about the beloved. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa (1.1.19) has a similar line: “we never tire of hearing the deeds of Uttamaśloka (Kṛṣṇa)”. The verse lies behind the tradition of listening to sacred stories again and again.",
 "Some things are worth hearing again and again. Return to what nourishes you.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Hanta te kathayiṣyāmi</i>, “yes, I will tell you”. <i>Divyā hy ātma-vibhūtayaḥ</i>, “my divine glories”. <i>Prādhānyataḥ kuru-śreṣṭha</i>, “the chief ones, best of the Kurus”. <i>Nāsty anto vistarasya me</i>, “for there is no end to my extent”.",
 "<i>Hanta</i>, an expression of warm agreement: “come, then”. Commentators note <i>prādhānyataḥ</i>, “by prominence”: Kṛṣṇa will name the chief one in each class. Śaṅkara says a complete list is impossible because the Lord is infinite. Rāmānuja says the chosen examples show the Lord’s presence in the best of each kind. The list that follows (10.20–39) draws on gods, sages, nature, animals, language, time and human qualities. It is a map for seeing the Divine everywhere.",
 "Notice excellence in each area of life. Each is a small window onto something greater.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Aham ātmā guḍākeśa</i>, “I am the Self, Guḍākeśa”. <i>Sarva-bhūtāśaya-sthitaḥ</i>, “seated in the heart of all beings”. <i>Aham ādiś ca madhyaṃ ca</i>, “I am the beginning and the middle”. <i>Bhūtānām anta eva ca</i>, “and the end of beings”.",
 "Before naming outer glories, Kṛṣṇa names the inner one: the Self in every heart. Commentators say this is the highest meditation; the outer glories are for those not yet able to meditate on the Self. Śaṅkara reads it as the one Self in all. Rāmānuja reads it as the Lord dwelling within each being as inner controller. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (2.1.4) calls the Lord the inner self of all beings. Beginning, middle and end: birth, life and death all rest in him. The verse returns in 13.17 and 15.15.",
 "The first place to look for the Divine is within. Sit quietly and turn toward the heart.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Ādityānām ahaṃ viṣṇur</i>, “of the Ādityas I am Viṣṇu”. <i>Jyotiṣāṃ ravir aṃśumān</i>, “of lights, the radiant sun”. <i>Marīcir marutām asmi</i>, “of the Maruts I am Marīci”. <i>Nakṣatrāṇām ahaṃ śaśī</i>, “of the stars, I am the moon”.",
 "The Ādityas are the twelve sons of Aditi, solar deities in the Vedas and Purāṇas. Viṣṇu is named among them, often identified as Vāmana, the youngest, who took the three strides. Commentators note this is the only place in the list where Kṛṣṇa names Viṣṇu, his own form. The Maruts are storm gods; Marīci is their chief or the light-ray among them. The <i>nakṣatras</i> are the twenty-seven lunar mansions; the moon passes through them each month. The pattern is: in each group, the brightest or most excellent.",
 "Look up at the sun or moon today. Let it be a reminder of the light behind all lights.")
